<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<section xml:id="port-instructions" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Portfolio Project Instructions - Math 408</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Your proof portfolio will consist of the statements and proofs of 7 theorems written according to the guidelines given below.  For each problem, you will be allowed to submit one draft for preliminary review.  The second draft will be your final draft, the only draft that receives a grade.  Each draft must be submitted electronically as a PDF document.  Here are some additional issues to keep in mind.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <!-- Include chapters -->
  <subsection xml:id="write-independently">
    <title>Your project must be written independently</title>
    <p>
      You may work collaboratively with other students on the problem solving portion of your project.  However, you must cite your collaborators on your paper, and you must write your solution by yourself without any aid.  Any violation of this policy is grounds for failure of the course.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="drafts">
    <title>Submitting drafts</title>
    <p>
      Each week on your homework assignment, you may include any work you have done on your portfolio problems in your portfolio report to get feedback from me.  In addition, for each problem, you may submit one formal draft on Blackboard for feedback.
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Each draft must be submitted electronically on Blackboard.  The draft for each problem must be in a separate file.  To submit a draft, go to the <q>Portfolio Project</q> learning module and click on the <q>Portfolio Rough Drafts</q> folder. I have links there for each portfolio problem.  Please use the link that corresponds to the problem you are submitting.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            I will do everything in my power to return your draft with comments.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            For your final draft (the one to be graded), you turn in all problems in a single file.  You can submit your final draft at the link in the <q>Portfolio Project</q> learning module.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="due-date">
    <title>Due Date</title>
    <p>
      Your project is due by the Wednesday of final exam week, on 12/16.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="format">
    <title>Format</title>
      <p>
        You must state your problem in the form of a theorem, followed by the proof.  Remember that a theorem is a mathematical statement, so your theorem should be written that way.  For example, if your portfolio problem asked you to <q>determine whether <m>ab</m> is odd or even, assuming that <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> are odd integers</q>, you might write something like the following.
      </p>
      <theorem xml:id="sample-theorem">
        <statement>
          <p>
            If <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> are odd integers, then <m>ab</m> is an odd integer.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <proof>
          <p>
            Assume that <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> are odd integers.  By the definition of odd integers, there are integers <m>k</m> and <m>m</m> such that <m>a=2k+1</m> and <m>b=2m+1</m>. We then have
            <md>
              <mrow>ab \amp = (2k+1)(2m+1)</mrow>
              <mrow>\amp = 4km +2k+2m+1</mrow>
              <mrow>\amp = 2(2km+k+m)+1</mrow>
            </md>
            Since <m>k</m> and <m>m</m> are integers and the integers are closed under addition and multiplication, we have that <m>2km+k+m</m> is an integer. By the definition of odd integers, we can conclude that <m>2(2km+k+m)+1</m> is odd, and so <m>ab</m> is odd.  This completes the proof.
          </p>
        </proof>
      </theorem>
  </subsection>
</section>
